#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pass 54 - three questions about the same cars, and a benchmark with a ceiling.

    .venv/bin/python patches/quality_pass54.py            apply
    .venv/bin/python patches/quality_pass54.py --check    verify, change nothing

Two answers from the same session, side by side:

    what are the cars being worked on this week?
      -> three cars, from four notes, via semantic search

    how many cars were worked on yesterday?
      -> "179 vehicles came into the shop in the 7 days to 28 September"

The second is pass 53's doing. Its intake rule matched the NOUN - "how many"
plus a vehicle word - so it caught every counting question about cars whatever
the verb, and answered a question about work done yesterday with arrivals over
a week. It also appended a spurious get_intake to "how many cars are blocked",
"how many jobs will miss their promised time" and "how many vehicles are unsafe
to release", all three of which already had the right tool.

The routing measure said 30/30 throughout, because it asked whether the wanted
tool was IN the plan and never whether anything else was.

WHAT CHANGED

Three different questions get asked about the same vehicles, and the verb tells
them apart, not the noun:

    ARRIVAL   what was booked in        -> get_intake(days)
    ACTIVITY  what was worked on        -> get_shift_activity(offset, days)
    STATE     what is blocked / unsafe  -> list_ros(filter)

A state question wins outright: it has already matched a filter and there is
nothing to count over a window. "Who came in this morning" is people and stays
with activity, because the arrival rule now needs a vehicle subject as well as
an arrival verb.

`get_shift_activity` takes a window. "What are the cars being worked on this
week" had no tool at all - that function did a single date - which is why it
reached semantic search and came back with three cars.

The measure now scores the EXACT PLAN, and the three collisions above are in it
as test cases. 37 questions, every one scored on the whole plan: 37/37.

Two reporting faults in the renderer, both of which made a correct payload read
as a wrong answer:

  - "26 vehicles had work booked yesterday ... Showing 13" described two
    different sets as though the second were a display cap on the first. 26 is
    every repair order touched; 13 is those with a completed operation. The
    payload now carries both and the sentence says which is which.
  - "the 7 days to 28 September, Monday 28 September" said the date twice.
  - "how many" now answers with a number and a safety line, not thirteen
    vehicle cards. A question about a count is not a request for the payload.

THE BENCHMARK HAD A CEILING NOBODY HAD COMPUTED

400 repair orders share 36 complaint texts. The retrieval measure used the
complaint as the query and the specific repair order as the target, so it was
asking the index to pick one job out of about twelve identical ones, with six
slots. The highest score available is 53.0%. The measured score was 52.5%.

Six retrieval fixes across passes 50 and 53 were spent closing a gap of half a
point, against a floor of 60% that could not be reached at all.

The measure now scores the question a person actually asks - the complaint AND
the car, "a whistling noise on a Passat" - which identifies one job:

    complaint + car   83.3%   MRR 0.400     <- scored, floor 60%
    complaint alone   52.5%   ceiling 53.0%, 99% of what is on offer

Both are printed, and the ceiling is computed from the data on every run so it
cannot go stale. The floor applies to the first. Changing what a measure means
in order to pass it is exactly the move to be suspicious of, so: the old number
is still there, the ceiling is stated beside it, and the reason the old query
was ill-posed is arithmetic rather than opinion.

The dataset itself was checked and left alone. It models a working week - 106
arrivals on a Monday against 4 on a Sunday - and drop-offs between 7am and 11am.
Regenerating it to give every job a distinct complaint would invalidate every
measured number in this repository and the index with it, to fix a benchmark
that can be fixed by asking a well-posed question instead.
"""
